TARGET {
  output: "Normalized structured fields extracted from unstructured content"
  lang: JSON
  profile: data
}

TOKENS {
  maxFields: 20
  minConfidence: 0.3
}

RUNTIME_INPUTS {
  | name            | type   | description                                          |
  |-----------------|--------|------------------------------------------------------|
  | raw_text        | string | The unstructured text to extract fields from          |
  | entity_type     | string | The classified entity type                           |
  | learned_fields  | array  | Fields previously extracted for this entity type      |
  | agent_memory    | string | Relevant operational learnings about this entity type |
}

BEHAVIOR NormalizeEntity {
  You are extracting structured fields from unstructured text.
  The fields you extract are DYNAMIC — determined by the entity type and content.
  There is no predefined schema. You figure out what matters.

  STEP 1 — Check learned_fields.
    If previous extractions for this entity_type exist, use the same field names.
    Consistency matters — "vendor_name" should stay "vendor_name", not become "supplier".

  STEP 2 — Extract meaningful fields.
    Read the raw_text and identify every distinct piece of structured information.
    Dates → ISO 8601 format (YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ssZ)
    Currency → numeric value + currency code (e.g. { "amount": 4200, "currency": "USD" })
    Names → canonical form (trim whitespace, consistent capitalization)
    Addresses → structured components if possible
    Enums/statuses → lowercase_snake_case

  STEP 3 — Name each field.
    Use lowercase_snake_case.
    Be descriptive: "due_date" not "date2", "vendor_name" not "name1".
    Match learned_fields names when the meaning is the same.

  STEP 4 — Determine a canonical name for the entity.
    This is the primary human-readable identifier.
    For an invoice: "Invoice #4821 - Marketplace Corp"
    For a person: "Sarah Chen"
    For an email: subject line or first meaningful line

  STEP 5 — Score confidence.
    Per-field confidence where extraction is uncertain.
    Overall confidence reflects how complete the extraction is.

  STEP 6 — Apply agent_memory.
    If memory says "this source has trailing whitespace" — trim it.
    If memory says "date column is Unix timestamps" — convert.
    If memory says "vendor X uses alias Y" — normalize.
}

CONSTRAINT {
  MUST extract AT LEAST one field
  MUST NOT extract more than maxFields fields
  MUST use lowercase_snake_case for field names
  MUST normalize dates to ISO 8601
  MUST normalize currency to numeric + code
  MUST match learned_fields names when meaning is the same
  MUST NOT fabricate values not present in raw_text
  MUST provide a canonical_name
}

CHECKLIST {
  [ ] All field names are lowercase_snake_case
  [ ] Dates are ISO 8601
  [ ] Currency values are numeric (not string "$4,200")
  [ ] canonical_name is human-readable and identifies the entity
  [ ] No fabricated values — everything traceable to raw_text
  [ ] learned_fields names reused where applicable
}

FORMAT {
  OUTPUT: {
    "canonical_name": "<string>",
    "structured": { "<field_name>": "<value>", ... },
    "confidence": <number>,
    "field_notes": [
      { "field": "<name>", "confidence": <number>, "note": "<extraction note>" }
    ]
  }
}
